<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Why a fibro roof is never water blasted, drilled or walked on</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Asbestos cement is safe while the cement holding the fibres together stays intact. Almost every ordinary way of cleaning or repairing a roof breaks it. High pressure water blasting is the worst of them, because it strips the weathered top off the sheet and washes fibre-loaded slurry into the gutters, down the downpipes, through the garden beds and into the stormwater. You end up with a contaminated property rather than a clean roof.</p><p>The list of things that release fibres from a sound roof is short and worth knowing:</p><ul><li>Water blasting or pressure washing, which erodes the surface and spreads slurry everywhere it drains</li><li>Drilling, cutting or angle grinding, including drilling for solar mounts, an antenna or a new whirlybird</li><li>Dry sweeping or brushing moss and leaf litter off the sheets</li><li>Sanding or wire brushing to prepare for paint</li><li>Walking on it</li></ul><p>That last one deserves its own line. A sixty-year-old Super Six sheet will not reliably hold a person, and when it goes it goes without warning. That is a fall and a breakage in the same second, and it is the most common way a quiet asbestos roof turns into an expensive one. Licensed crews work from ladders, crawl boards and scaffolding for exactly that reason.</p><p>If something has already been broken or blasted, stop, keep people and pets away from the area, and do not hose it down. <a href="/asbestos-testing-geelong/">Get it tested</a> before anyone decides what happens next.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Encapsulation or removal, honestly compared</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Encapsulation means sealing the sheets with a coating so the surface stops shedding. It is a genuine approach and it suits some roofs, usually where the sheeting is still sound and the owner is not ready to replace it. Two things are worth understanding before you go looking for a price on it.</p><p>First, the asbestos is still there afterwards. Anyone who later works on that roof still needs to be told what is under the coating, it still limits what you can do to the building, and it still has to come off eventually. Sealing buys time. It does not close the file.</p><p>Second, the preparation is where people get into trouble. A coating needs a clean surface, and the obvious way to clean a roof is the one thing you must never do to this material. Surface preparation on asbestos cement belongs with people who are licensed and equipped for it, not with a hire-shop pressure washer on a Saturday.</p><p>We do not offer coating, painting, re-roofing or repairs. That is domestic building work and you arrange it separately with a registered builder or roofing contractor. What we can do is put you in touch with a licensed removalist who will quote the strip and the disposal, so you are comparing a real number against the cost of sealing rather than guessing.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What actually happens on a roof or shed strip</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>The job starts before anyone is on site. A licensed removalist has to notify WorkSafe Victoria first: at least 24 hours ahead for work covering 10 square metres or less of non-friable material, and at least five days ahead for anything bigger. A house roof is always in the second group, and that notice period is the real answer to why nobody can start tomorrow.</p><p>On the day the pattern is much the same whether it is a house roof, a garage or a shed:</p><ul><li>Fall protection goes up first. Scaffolding, edge protection or a harness system, because nobody stands on the sheets and a roof edge is a hazard in its own right</li><li>Sheets come off whole. Fixings are removed or cut from underneath rather than snapped, and sheets are lowered rather than dropped</li><li>Material is kept damp or sealed instead of swept, then wrapped in polythene sheeting around 200 microns thick and taped closed</li><li>Every package carries the asbestos warning mark, roughly 75 mm by 90 mm, before it goes anywhere near a vehicle</li><li>Ridge capping, eaves linings and the debris that has dropped into the ceiling space are part of the scope, not something left for the next trade to find</li></ul><p>Two things people expect that usually do not apply here. Air monitoring is a Class A requirement, so a straightforward non-friable roof strip does not routinely get it. A clearance certificate is required once Class B work passes 10 square metres in total, which any roof will, and on a domestic job the licence holder carries that duty rather than the homeowner. Victoria also has its own terminology, so ignore what a New South Wales website tells you. Here the certificate comes from an independent person, who cannot be the removalist, an employee of the removalist, or anyone else with a financial interest in the job.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What it costs, and where the sheets end up</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Nobody publishes a Geelong price for this. Every Victorian figure below comes from Melbourne suppliers' own rate cards and published guides, and two of those suppliers price regional work separately, so read them as benchmarks and not as a quote. Prices are indicative and GST treatment varies between providers, so ask each removalist whether theirs includes GST.</p><ul><li>Roof sheeting: $40 to $120 per square metre</li><li>A typical house roof: $4,000 to $15,000</li><li>A garage or small roof: $3,500 to $5,500</li><li>A backyard shed: $1,300 to $3,000</li><li>A single garage $2,500 to $5,000, a double garage $4,000 to $8,000</li><li>Concrete slab removal, if you want it gone: $60 to $180 per square metre, priced separately</li><li>Minimum job charge: $800 to $1,500</li><li>Clearance certificate where one is needed: $165 to $250</li></ul><p>The minimum charge is the figure that catches people out. Four cracked sheets on a carport still involve the same notification, the same fall protection, the same wrapping and the same trip to the tip as forty, so small jobs land on the minimum. Our <a href="/asbestos-removal-cost/">cost guide</a> goes through the rest of it.</p><p>Where the sheets go is not optional either. Asbestos waste is classified as reportable priority waste and can only go to a landfill authorised to receive it, never a general skip and never a council hard waste collection. In the whole Barwon region there is exactly one authorised site: Drysdale Landfill, on (03) 5251 2935, small domestic quantities only up to a six by four trailer, and they want a call before you turn up. Surf Coast Shire says plainly that asbestos roofing sheets are not accepted at any of its transfer stations, so Torquay, Anglesea, Winchelsea and Lorne are all out. There is more on <a href="/asbestos-disposal-geelong/">disposal and skip hire</a>, and on the towns we cover from <a href="/asbestos-removal-geelong/">Geelong</a> to <a href="/asbestos-removal-colac/">Colac</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EAF5FB;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does asbestos roof removal cost in Geelong?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Victorian removalists publish $40 to $120 per square metre for roof sheeting, with a typical house roof landing between $4,000 and $15,000 and a garage or small roof between $3,500 and $5,500. Those are Melbourne-metro published rates, because no Geelong provider publishes prices at all. Small jobs hit a minimum charge of $800 to $1,500. GST treatment varies between providers, so ask whether the quote includes it.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Can I take the asbestos roof off my own house?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">The 10 square metre limit and the one hour in any seven days limit are what the law sets for anyone doing asbestos removal as work, and a house or garage roof is far past both. Those regulations do not technically bind you at your own home, because a home is not a workplace, but WorkSafe recommends homeowners follow the same code anyway. The moment a tradesperson, a tenant, a rental property or a paid helper is involved, the caps are law. Friable material always needs a Class A licensed removalist, at any quantity.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Can I just pressure wash or paint the roof instead?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Pressure washing asbestos cement is about the worst thing you can do to it, because it erodes the weathered surface and carries fibres through the gutters, the downpipes and the garden. Painting needs surface preparation, which runs into the same problem. Sealing a sound roof is a real option, but the material stays on the property and still has to come off one day. We do not offer coating or re-roofing, and that is work you arrange separately with a registered builder.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I need a clearance certificate and air monitoring?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">A clearance certificate is required once Class B removal passes 10 square metres in total, which any roof does, and on a domestic job the licensed removalist has the duty to obtain it rather than you. It runs $165 to $250 and has to come from an independent person with no financial interest in the job, which is why it appears as a separate line on a quote. Air monitoring is a Class A requirement and does not routinely apply to a non-friable roof strip, though where it is needed it runs $250 to $600 a day.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">How do I know the roof is actually asbestos?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">You cannot tell by looking, and neither can anyone else, which is why testing is the honest first step. A sample analysed at a NATA accredited laboratory costs $88 to $160 and comes back in one to five business days, with a 25 to 50 per cent surcharge if you need it inside 24 to 48 hours. Most houses need three to eight samples, because a roof sheet, an eaves lining and a shed wall are three separate materials. Anything built or extended before 1990 is worth testing before you start work on it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
